    description: "Tracker TUI feature: in the Description and Activity panes, detect substrings that look like item IDs and make them clickable. Clicking opens a modal that occludes 80-90% of the left (list) pane and displays the target item's full details (Title, ID, Status, Priority, Description, Activity — everything the right-side Description + Activity panes normally show). The modal has four controls: (1) a text entry at the top to jump to an arbitrary item by ID, (2) Back, (3) Forward, (4) Close.\n\nKey design constraints\n\n1. ID pattern is config-driven, NOT hardcoded. Prefixes come from TrackerConfig.kinds (a dict of kind_name → KindConfig.prefix). Today those are WI/INV/META, but custom kinds with custom prefixes are supported and the feature must handle any configured prefix. Construct the regex at TUI startup (or on config reload) as: `(?:<prefix1>|<prefix2>|…)-(?:[bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz][aeiou][bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz][aeiou][bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz])(?:-[bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz][aeiou][bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz][aeiou][bcdfghjklmnpqrstvz])+` — a proquint block followed by one-or-more hyphen-separated proquint blocks, to match current 8-syllable IDs and any historical variants. See store.py docstring at line ~373 for the ID format.\n\n2. Detection is lazy / best-effort. Scan only the currently-visible lines in each pane; don't scan the whole item set eagerly. Cache matches per (item_id, pane, scroll_position) if needed. Display each match as a clickable hotspot (underlined or highlighted per the TUI's existing link style). A match that doesn't resolve to an existing item renders as normal text (not a dead hotspot).\n\n3. Modal layout: covers 80-90% of the left list pane's width × height, preserving the right panes' visibility for context. Stack order: modal on top of list pane; right panes remain interactive for the ITEM BEHIND the modal (the originating context), not for the item displayed in the modal.\n\n4. Modal content mirrors the right-side panes: show Title header + ID + Status + Priority + tier + created/updated; Description (rendered same as the main Description pane); Activity (rendered same as the main Activity pane, including unread markers and authorship). Item IDs within the modal's Description/Activity content should also be clickable, pushing a new entry onto the navigation stack.\n\n5. Navigation stack semantics: Back / Forward behave like a browser history. Closing the modal discards the stack. Entering a new ID in the text entry appends to the stack (truncating any Forward history — also browser-like). Invalid ID typed in the entry field shows an inline error, does not push to stack.\n\n6. Close button dismisses the modal and returns focus to whatever had it before the modal opened (the originating Description or Activity pane, typically).\n\n7. Mouse click is the primary activation, but also expose a keyboard path: Tab through visible hotspots + Enter to activate (accessibility and SSH-over-mouseless sessions).\n\nOut-of-scope (file follow-up items if we want them)\n\n- Real-time re-scan when an item's content changes via a discuss op landing during modal open.\n- Syntax highlighting within the modal's Description for code blocks.\n- A \"pin\" feature that keeps the modal open across navigation. (MVP is single-modal with history stack.)\n\nImplementation location\n\n- Tracker TUI lives in packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/tui.py. Likely uses Textual widgets (check current imports). Any new modal probably needs a ModalScreen subclass plus a widget for the input-with-history row.\n\nWhy now\n\nUser requested on 2026-04-17 to improve tracker navigation ergonomics. Current workflow requires copy-pasting an ID into a separate shell to run `tracker show` — moderate friction compounded across every cross-reference a human follows."  # 7cde
